[837.192, "\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\u001b[1C\u001b[1B\u001b[1mEdit file\u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;102;102;102mNOTES.md\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;175;175;175m\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\u254c\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m\u001b[2m 22 \u001b[22m   **column-tiled transposed layout** `(tiles, in, 128)`. Tile `t` holds output rows\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m\u001b[2m 23 \u001b[22m   `128t \u2026 128t+127` of `W`, stored input-major. Values are unchanged float32 and only the layout\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m\u001b[2m 24 \u001b[22m   differs. The q/k/v tiles share one array per layer, and so do the w1/w3 tiles. The classifier\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;207;34;46m\u001b[48;2;255;220;220m 25 -\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  gets its own tiled copy (it is shared with the embedding "]
[837.193, "in this checkpoint). \u001b[48;2;255;199;199mThe\u001b[48;2;255;220;220m \u001b[48;2;255;199;199membedding table\u001b[48;2;255;220;220m                \r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;207;34;46m 26 -\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  is also kept in its original row layout for the token lookup. A dimension that isn't a multiple                  \r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;207;34;46m 27 -\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  of 128 is zero-padded to whole tiles. The padded outputs are never read.                                         \r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;36;138;61m\u001b[48;2;220;255;220m 25 +\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  gets its own tiled copy (it is shared with the embedding in this checkpoint). \u001b[48;2;178;255;178mWith\u001b[48;2;220;255;220m \u001b[48;2;178;255;178mshared\u001b[48;2;220;255;220m                        \r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;36;138;61m 26 +\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  weights there is no separate embedding copy: `embed()` reads row `token` out of the tiled                        \r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;36;138;61m 27 +\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  classifier (the same float32 values). An unshared embedding gets its own tiled copy. A dimension                 \r\u001b[1B\u001b["]
[837.193, "38;2;36;138;61m 28 +\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m  that isn't a multiple of 128 is zero-padded to whole tiles. The padded outputs are never read.                   \r\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[2m 29 \u001b[22m - **Forward pass** (`forward`, all `@njit` nopython, `cache=True`, no `fastmath`, no `parallel`):\r\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 30 \u001b[22m   - same ops as run.c, in the same order: rmsnorm \u2192 q/k/v \u2192 RoPE \u2192 attention with softmax over\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;51;51;51m\u001b[2m 31 \u001b[22m     0..pos \u2192 wo \u2192 residual \u2192 rmsnorm \u2192 w1/w3 \u2192 SwiGLU \u2192 w2 \u2192 residual \u2192 final rmsnorm \u2192\u001b[39m\u001b[40;1H\u001b[36;2H"]
